\begin{center}
\Large\textbf{A Parameter-Free Geometric Model for Particle Masses: \\ Worldline Filaments, the Time Wavefront, and Emergent Constants}
\end{center}

\begin{abstract}
The Scalar–Angular–Torsion (SAT) theory proposes that reality is a four-dimensional tangled network of particle worldlines called \textbf{filaments} [2, 3]. This framework provides a covariant geometric formalism utilizing four fundamental fields, adhering strictly to the \textbf{Normalization Principle} (Zero-Parameter Economy) [4, 5]. All dimensionless constants and particle masses follow uniquely from the geometry after fixing a single physical anchor [4, 6]. The core dynamics are derived from a single, unified action [4, 7]. We derive the unified Euler–Lagrange system, focusing on the stability requirements of the emergent time-flow vector, $\mathbf{u}^\mu$ [8, 9]. The topological mass mechanism achieves a light-sector RMS residual error of $\mathbf{0.55\%}$ [10, 11], and the gauge couplings unify to within $\mathbf{4.5\%}$ accuracy at $\mu_U \approx 3.1 \times 10^{17}$ GeV [12-14].
\end{abstract}

\section{Postulates and Core Geometric Fields}

The SAT framework begins on a 4-dimensional differentiable manifold $\mathcal M$ without an a priori metric [15, 16]. The basic primitives (fields) emerge from filament dynamics [15, 17]:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Unit Time-Flow Vector ($\mathbf{u}^\mu$):} Defines the dynamic time foliation ($\Sigma_t$) as the gradient of a scalar time field $T(x)$, enforced to satisfy $\mathbf{u}^\mu \mathbf{u}_\mu = -1$ [17-19]. Fluctuations $\delta \mathbf{u}^\mu$ are termed \textbf{Temporons}, which generate induced inertial mass ($\mathbf{m}_{\text{ind}}$) [18, 20].
    \item \textbf{Misalignment Field ($\mathbf{\theta_4}$):} Represents the angle/tension between the particle filament and the advancing time surface, governing geometric mass acquisition [20, 21].
    \item \textbf{Internal Phase Field ($\mathbf{\psi}$):} A compact-phase field whose symmetry rotation leads to the emergent $\mathbf{U(1)}$ and $\mathbf{SU(2)}$ gauge sectors [22].
    \item \textbf{Topological Twist Field ($\mathbf{\tau}$):} A discrete $\mathbf{\mathbb{Z}_3}$-valued field that enforces topological conservation laws and gives rise to $\mathbf{SU(3)}$ color confinement [22, 23].
\end{itemize}

\section{Unified Action and Geometric Lagrangian Density (Revised)}
\label{sec:action}

The dynamics are governed by the Total Action $\mathbf{S}_{\text{SAT}}$ [24]. This action integrates all fields into a minimal Effective Field Theory (EFT) Lagrangian density, adhering to the axiomatic constraint that gravity, matter, and gauge sectors arise from geometric tension and topological coupling [25, 26].

The covariant Unified Action for the fully dynamical fields $[E,u,\psi,\theta_4,\tau,A,\Psi,\Phi]$ is defined as [7, 27]:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:full_action_covariant}
\mathbf{S}_{\text{SAT}}[E,u,\psi,\theta_4,\tau,A,\Psi,\Phi]
= \int_M \!\! d^4x\; \sqrt{-g}\;
\Biggl[
\frac{c^4}{16\pi G_{\text{ind}}}\,R[g]
+ \mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{u}}
+ \mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{\theta\psi}}
+ \mathcal{L}_{\text{gauge}}
+ \mathcal{L}_{\text{Dirac}}
+ \mathcal{L}_{\text{int}}
\Biggr]\;+\; S_{\text{Top}}.
\end{equation}

In the non-relativistic limit, the dynamics are driven by a corrected Lagrangian density $\mathcal L$, which carries energy density units $[M L^{-1} T^{-2}]$ [28, 29]:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lagrangian_non_rel}
\mathcal L = \frac{1}{2}\rho \mathbf v^2 - \rho\,W(\Phi) - \frac{\kappa}{2}(\nabla\Phi)^2 + \lambda\,\rho\,\Phi\, (\nabla\cdot\mathbf v). \quad [29]
\end{equation}
The Lagrangian density is dimensionally consistent, where $W(\Phi)$ is the potential per unit mass and $\kappa$ is the curvature coupling $[\kappa]=M L^{-3} T^{2}$ [28, 30].

\section{Structural Integrity and Stability (Constraint Formalism)}

The coherence of the SAT theory critically depends on ensuring the classical stability and ghost-free propagation of the $\mathbf{u}^\mu$ fluctuations (Temporons) [31, 32].

\subsection*{Ghost-Free Dynamics}
The mandatory normalization constraint $\mathbf{u}^\mu \mathbf{u}_\mu = -1$ [19, 31] requires a rigorous **Dirac–Bergmann constraint analysis** [9, 33] to confirm that the constraint algebra closes, yielding a second-class system [34, 35]. To eliminate unphysical longitudinal ghost modes, the kinetic terms governing $\mathbf{u}^\mu$ must be re-expressed using the **spatial projector** $\mathbf{P}^{\mu\nu}$ [33, 35, 36]:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:projector_formalism}
\mathbf{P}^{\mu\nu} = g^{\mu\nu} + \mathbf{u}^\mu \mathbf{u}^\nu \quad \text{(Orthogonal Projector)} \quad [35, 37]
\end{equation}
This projects the dynamics onto the three-dimensional subspace orthogonal to $\mathbf{u}^\mu$, ensuring stability [34, 38].

\section{Governing Euler–Lagrange Field Equations}

Variation of the action yields coupled differential equations. The full covariant equations, including the Dirac and Maxwell/Yang-Mills forms, are derived in the covariant modules [39, 40].

\subsection*{Non-Relativistic System Summary}
From variation of the constrained non-relativistic action ($S[\rho,\mathbf v,\Phi,\chi]$) [41]:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Mass Continuity (from $\delta S / \delta \chi$):} Enforces mass conservation [42-44]:
    \[
    \boxed{ \; \partial_t\rho + \nabla\!\cdot(\rho\mathbf v) = 0 \; . \;}
    \]
    \item \textbf{Momentum Balance (Convective Form):} Derived from Clebsch-style parametrization and material time derivative $D_t \equiv \partial_t + \mathbf v\!\cdot\!\nabla$ [44-46]:
    \[
    \rho\big(\partial_t\mathbf v + (\mathbf v\!\cdot\!\nabla)\mathbf v\big)
    = -\nabla P - \rho\,\nabla W(\Phi) - \kappa\,(\nabla^2\Phi)\,\nabla\Phi + \lambda\,\nabla(\rho\Phi). \quad [43, 44]
    \]
    The term $\mathbf f_\kappa = -\kappa\,(\nabla^2\Phi)\,\nabla\Phi$ is the curvature body force [46, 47].
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Covariant Dirac Equation}
The field equation for the filamentary spinor field $\Psi$ (fermions) is derived from $\mathcal{L}_{\text{Dirac}}$ [40, 48]:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dirac_eom}
\boxed{ \bigl( i\hbar\,\gamma^\mu D_\mu - m_\Psi(x)c \bigr)\,\Psi = 0. } \quad [40]
\end{equation}
The mass term $m_\Psi(x)$ is an emergent property derived from the geometry [49].

\section{Geometric Origin of Mass and Unification}

\subsection*{Mass Hierarchy}
Particle mass is not intrinsic but an emergent property encoded in filament geometry and topology [4, 20]. The measured mass ($\mathbf{m}_{\text{eff}}$) is the sum of two components [50, 51]:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:topo_mass_final}
\mathbf{m}_{\text{eff}} = m_{\text{topo}} + m_{\text{ind}} = \frac{m_0}{Q} \;+\; C\;\frac{g_1^2}{M^2}\;E_{\text{overlap}} \quad [50-52]
\end{equation}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Topological Mass ($\mathbf{m}_{\text{topo}}$):} The dominant component, inversely proportional to the dimensionless Topological Charge ($\mathbf{Q}$) [53, 54]. $\mathbf{Q}$ quantifies filament linking, writhe, and winding complexity [55].
    \item \textbf{Induced Inertial Mass ($\mathbf{m}_{\text{ind}}$):} Generated by the drag/coupling interactions with the Temporon fluctuations ($\mathbf{\delta u}^\mu$) [20, 53].
\end{itemize}
The theory requires the **Topological Dominance Condition** ($\mathbf{R} \equiv \mathbf{m}_{\text{ind}} / \mathbf{m}_{\text{topo}} \ll 1$) for consistency [50, 56]. Stability is constrained by the **UV Finiteness Lock**, which mandates that only bundles with integer topological charge $\mathbf{Q \leq 3}$ can be dynamically stable [56, 57].

\subsection*{Geometric Gauge Unification}
The gauge group $\mathbf{SU(3) \times SU(2) \times U(1)}$ is structurally derived from filament topology and phase structure [22, 58].

\begin{itemize}
    \item **Coupling Emergence:** The inverse coupling squared $g_G^{-2}$ is a local function of the topological linking density ($\rho_G(x)$) and the filament scale ($\ell_f$) [59, 60]: $g_G^{-2}(x) \sim \rho_G(x) \cdot \ell_f^2$ [60].
    \item **Unification Precision:** Evaluating the two-loop RG evolution based on these emergent couplings predicts that the gauge couplings unify to within $\mathbf{4.5\%}$ accuracy at the high unification scale ($\mathbf{\mu_U \approx 3.1 \times 10^{17}}$ GeV) [12-14].
\end{itemize}

\section{Appendix Summary: Extended Derivations}

In line with submission guidelines, extensive derivations are relegated to Supplemental Material [61, 62].

\subsection*{A. Non-Relativistic Derivations}
The full Euler–Lagrange derivations for the corrected SAT Lagrangian are available, including the derivation of the Bernoulli (generalized Hamilton–Jacobi) relation for the Lagrange multiplier $\chi$ [63, 64] and the confirmation of the total energy identity $dE_{\text{tot}}/dt = 0$ when damping is absent [47, 65].

\subsection*{B. Stability and Rigor}
The proofs for classical stability and quantum consistency of the emergent fields are formalized using high-rigor mathematical tools [34, 66]. These include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item **Dirac–Bergmann Algorithm:** Required to analyze the constraint algebra of the $\mathbf{u}^\mu$ field dynamics, securing the classical stability of the emergent Temporon mode ($\delta \mathbf{u}^\mu$) [34, 35].
    \item **Renormalizability:** The theory is power-counting finite, constrained by the topological geometry which ensures that two renormalization constants suffice at all loop orders, ensuring UV completeness and asymptotic safety [67, 68].
\end{itemize}

\end{document}

